broadcom/compiler: make liveness cheap enough to run after every spill

The V3D register allocator spills one temporary at a time and rebuilds
liveness (vir_calculate_live_intervals) after each spill. Godot's Mobile
renderer has per-pixel-lit fragment shaders of ~14k instructions that
spill ~650 times per strategy, and the 2-thread strategies are all tried
in search of the fewest spills. Liveness was ~80% of compile time, in
two places:

- the defin/defout propagation pushes definitions to successors but
  walked the blocks in reverse, so it needed about one full pass per
  level of CFG depth to converge; walk in program order;
- vir_compute_start_end tested every temp in every block, and
  vir_setup_def_use cleared a per-temp array for every block; visit
  only the set bits.

The results are unchanged: every shader in the test scene compiles to
the same QPU code (hashed) as before.

Cold first frame of a Mobile-renderer scene (shadowed directional, omni
and spot lights, fog, glow, PBR textures), V3D 7.1 under drm-shim on
x86-64, Mesa 25.2.8 debugoptimized, shader cache off:

  stock                                 173 s
  this patch                             58-64 s
  this patch + V3D_DEBUG=opt_compile_time 13 s

Applies to Mesa 25.x (src/broadcom/compiler/vir_live_variables.c).

--- a/src/broadcom/compiler/vir_live_variables.c
+++ b/src/broadcom/compiler/vir_live_variables.c
